    2 27D4              ; variable count, sixteen bits and thirty-two.
    3 27D4              ;
    4 27D4              ; The compiler expands what it can inline and calls in here for the rest, under the ordinary
    5 27D4              ; compiler's calling convention: a sixteen-bit operation takes its operands in HL and DE
    6 27D4              ; and returns in HL; a thirty-two-bit one takes the first operand's low word in HL and high word
    7 27D4              ; in DE and the second's in BC and on the stack above the return address (or a count in BC), and
    8 27D4              ; returns in HL (low) and DE (high). So these are ordinary functions as far as the compiler is
    9 27D4              ; concerned.
   10 27D4              ;
   11 27D4              ; Only what a program uses is assembled: every routine is inside IFUSED, so an image carries the
   12 27D4              ; routines it calls and the ones those call, and nothing else. sjasmplus decides that in one pass
   13 27D4              ; only if every routine comes after all the routines that call it -- the order below -- which is
   14 27D4              ; also why the thirty-two-bit routines, which call the sixteen-bit ones, come first. They used to
   15 27D4              ; be in runtime32.asm; that file is now empty and kept so that command lines naming it still work.
   16 27D4              ;
   17 27D4              ; Division of longs keeps its operands in static memory, because it needs a dividend, a divisor
   18 27D4              ; and a remainder of four bytes each and the machine has three pairs, and so does the floating
   19 27D4              ; point. An interrupt handler gets a copy of its own of this whole file, every name of it NAME__i,
   20 27D4              ; which x80cc makes when it links a program whose handlers call into here: the handler never runs
   21 27D4              ; inside the program's routines, nor in their working space. What the two share is between a line ";@shared" and one ";@end", which the
   22 27D4              ; copy leaves out.
   23 27D4              ;
   24 27D4              ; Sixty-four-bit arithmetic takes its operands by address, the result written over the first
   25 27D4              ; operand, since eight bytes fit in no set of registers; see the comment above those routines.
   26 27D4              ;
   27 27D4              ; Written by hand and measured rather than guessed at: every routine is checked against the same
